# 9. Constructor

A **constructor is a special function that automatically executes when an object is created.**

Rules:

* Same name as class
* No return type
* Automatically called

```cpp
class Student {
public:

    Student() {
        cout << "Constructor called";
    }
};
```

When:

```cpp
Student s;
```

Output:

```text
Constructor called
```

---

## Parameterized constructor

```cpp
class Student {
public:
    string name;
    int age;

    Student(string n, int a) {
        name = n;
        age = a;
    }
};
```

```cpp
Student s("Rajveer", 22);
```

---

## Constructor overloading

```cpp
class Student {
public:

    Student() {
        cout << "Default";
    }

    Student(string name) {
        cout << name;
    }
};
```

This is constructor overloading.

---

# 10. Destructor

A **destructor is automatically called when an object is destroyed.**

Syntax:

```cpp
~ClassName()
```

Example:

```cpp
class Student {
public:

    Student() {
        cout << "Constructor\n";
    }

    ~Student() {
        cout << "Destructor\n";
    }
};
```

```cpp
int main() {

    Student s;

}
```

Output:

```text
Constructor
Destructor
```

### Constructor vs Destructor

| Constructor                | Destructor                   |
| -------------------------- | ---------------------------- |
| Initializes object         | Cleans up object             |
| Same name as class         | `~` + class name             |
| Called when object created | Called when object destroyed |
| Can have parameters        | Cannot have parameters       |
| Can be overloaded          | Cannot be overloaded         |

