# 20. Important: Overloading vs Overriding

This is a **very common interview question**.

### Overloading

Same function name, **different parameters**, generally in the same class.

```cpp
class Calculator {
public:
    int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    int add(int a, int b, int c) {
        return a + b + c;
    }
};
```

### Overriding

Parent and child have the **same function signature**.

```cpp
class Parent {
public:
    virtual void show() {
        cout << "Parent";
    }
};

class Child : public Parent {
public:
    void show() override {
        cout << "Child";
    }
};
```

| Overloading               | Overriding                    |
| ------------------------- | ----------------------------- |
| Same class usually        | Parent-child relationship     |
| Different parameters      | Same signature                |
| Compile time              | Runtime with virtual dispatch |
| Compile-time polymorphism | Runtime polymorphism          |

---

# 21. Composition

Composition means a class **contains an object of another class**.

Example:

```cpp
class Engine {
public:
    void start() {
        cout << "Engine started";
    }
};

class Car {
private:
    Engine engine;

public:
    void startCar() {
        engine.start();
    }
};
```

Here:

```text
Car
 |
 └── Engine
```

A `Car` **has an** `Engine`.

This is called a **has-a relationship**.

---

# 22. Inheritance vs Composition

### Inheritance

**IS-A relationship**

```text
Dog IS-A Animal
```

```cpp
class Dog : public Animal {};
```

### Composition

**HAS-A relationship**

```text
Car HAS-A Engine
```

```cpp
class Car {
    Engine engine;
};
```

Interviewers often ask this distinction.

