## ⭐ Most important for interviews

If the interviewer says **"Explain OOPs"**, don't start explaining 20 concepts individually.

Give this answer:

> **OOP, or Object-Oriented Programming, is a programming paradigm where we organize software around objects that contain data and behavior. The four main principles are encapsulation, abstraction, inheritance, and polymorphism. Encapsulation protects and controls access to data, abstraction hides implementation details, inheritance allows code reusability through parent-child relationships, and polymorphism allows the same interface or function to behave differently in different situations. C++ supports these concepts using classes, objects, access specifiers, inheritance, virtual functions, abstract classes, function overloading, and operator overloading.**

Then, if they ask for examples, use **Employee Management System**:

```text
Employee
   |
   ├── Developer
   ├── Manager
   └── HR

Employee → parent class
Developer/Manager → inheritance
private/protected data → encapsulation
abstract functions → abstraction
calculateBonus() differently → polymorphism
```

This is a very good example to use in a **C++ interview** because you can demonstrate almost every major OOP concept from one system.
